alex_new: Hi all — first week! Trying to run the quickstart sample against the hosted endpoint but keep getting 429s. I'm using the key from the onboarding page (`REDWOOD_API_KEY`) and a small Node script. Any pointers? :wave:

liz_devex: Welcome Alex! :tada: Onboarding keys are usually sandbox keys unless you set up billing. Sandbox has QPS and token caps. Can you paste the exact curl/node response (headers included)?

alex_new: Here's the response header snippet I grabbed: `HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests` and `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0`, `x-ratelimit-reset: 14`. The script is doing about 15 short requests/sec while testing streaming.

mike_sre: That pattern will hit sandbox QPS quickly. Sandbox default is ~5 rps per key last I checked. Also streaming by opening many short connections is worse than one stream. Try batching tokens into one request or use SSE/streaming with a single connection.

liz_devex: Also check if your CI or teammates picked up the same sandbox key accidentally. Sandbox keys are per-user but if pasted in a shared script they deplete fast. In Console > API keys you can see last used time and client IPs.

alex_new: Good call — I accidentally had the key in a shared gist. Removed it now. Is there a quick temporary increase route for a demo tomorrow? I need roughly 25 rps for 10 minutes to demo concurrent streams.

liz_devex: We can grant short-lived demo boosts. Please DM me the key id and exact UTC window and I'll file a request. The team prefers the template in the ops-bot reply (it creates a ticket automatically).

ops-bot: Sandbox increase form: include `key_id, start_time_utc -> end_time_utc, expected_rps, reason`. Example: `alex-sandbox-2, 2026-04-02T17:00Z -> 2026-04-02T17:10Z, 25rps, onboarding demo`.

mike_sre: For local resilience, add client-side rate limiting and exponential jitter. Example (Node): ```js
// simple retry with jitter
const backoff = (attempt) => Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attempt, 10000) + Math.random() * 200;
for (let i=0;i<5;i++){
  const res = await fetch(...);
  if (res.status === 200) break;
  if (res.status === 429) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff(i)));
}
```

alex_new: Thanks — I'll DM the details and schedule. Quick conceptual Q: do sandbox keys count against org hosted capacity or just my user sandbox quota?

mike_sre: Sandbox keys are isolated per-user and do not count against org hosted capacity. Hosted keys (when billing is active) are account-level and can have shared quotas depending on plan.

liz_devex: One last tip: when testing streaming, use a single connection with larger max_tokens to simulate load rather than spinning up many short-lived connections. That reduces QPS pressure.

alex_new: Perfect. Appreciate the help! :pray: I'll DM now and remove the gist link from my repo. ``
